INT. ROOFTOP PIGEON LOFT - DAY

A plywood shed bolted to a tar roof, six floors above a bus depot. Wire cages line the walls. Feathers drift in the light from a cracked skylight.

TEODORO ABARCA, 70s, thick forearms, a flat cap gone gray at the brim, kneels on an overturned bucket. He holds a speckled pigeon against his chest, checks its leg band, sets it in a crate, picks up the next bird, checks that band, and sets it in the same crate. A clipboard hangs from a nail beside him. He does not look at it.

The door bangs open.

His granddaughter MILAGROS, 10, braids, a school backpack bigger than her torso, stands in the doorway breathing hard from the stairs.

MILAGROS
You said Thursday.

TEODORO
It is Thursday.

MILAGROS
You said after school Thursday.

TEODORO
School is over.

MILAGROS
Not for me. I ran from the bus.

She drops the backpack, crosses to the crate, and puts both hands on the lid. Teodoro lifts another bird and she snatches the bird from his hands, tucks it under her arm, and backs away toward the cages while he stays on the bucket with his empty fingers still curled.

TEODORO
Give him here.

MILAGROS
His name is Pavarotti.

TEODORO
His name is four-one-seven. The man is coming at five.

MILAGROS
Then he can come and I'll be sitting on the crate.

TEODORO
Milagros.

MILAGROS
Mamá said you were going to ask me.

TEODORO
I am asking you to put the bird in the box.

MILAGROS
That's not asking.

Teodoro stands, knees cracking. He takes off the flat cap, wipes his forehead with the back of his wrist, puts the cap on again. Milagros steps up onto the bucket he left so she is nearly his height, the bird still pinned under her arm, and stares at him while the other pigeons shuffle and coo behind the wire.

TEODORO
Your grandmother kept forty birds up here.

MILAGROS
I know.

TEODORO
Forty. I have eleven.

MILAGROS
Twelve. Pavarotti is the twelfth.

TEODORO
Eleven that fly. Pavarotti has a wing that does not close.

MILAGROS
Because nobody lets him practice.

TEODORO
(quietly)
He practiced for two years.

MILAGROS
Then the man can take the other eleven.

TEODORO
The man wants twelve. It is a lot, twelve. He is paying for twelve.

She steps down. She walks the length of the loft with the bird, past the water trays and the sack of feed with its mouth rolled shut, and stops at the skylight. She holds the bird up into the light. It opens one wing and the other hangs crooked, as Teodoro said. She lowers it. Teodoro has followed her down the aisle and stands a pace behind with one hand on a cage frame.

MILAGROS
If you sell them you'll have nothing to do on the roof.

TEODORO
I will come up and look at the sky.

MILAGROS
You'll hate it.

TEODORO
Probably.

MILAGROS
Then why?

TEODORO
The landlord says birds on the roof are a fee. The fee is more than the pension.

MILAGROS
You never said that.

TEODORO
I am saying it.

MILAGROS
How much more?

TEODORO
Enough.

MILAGROS
How much, Abuelo?

Teodoro opens his mouth, closes it, then takes the clipboard off its nail and holds it out. She reads it with her lips moving, bird tucked under one arm, and her free hand comes up and wipes her nose with her sleeve.

MILAGROS (CONT'D)
That's not that much.

TEODORO
It is every month.

MILAGROS
I have forty dollars.

TEODORO
Keep your forty dollars.

MILAGROS
And Mamá has some.

TEODORO
No.

MILAGROS
And Tío Rafa.

TEODORO
No.

She hangs the clipboard back on its nail and turns to the crate. She lifts the lid, looks at the eleven birds inside shifting against one another, lowers the lid, and sits on it, bird in her lap, backpack kicked against her ankle. Teodoro lets out a breath through his nose and sits on the bucket across from her, knees almost touching hers.

MILAGROS (CONT'D)
I'm not moving.

TEODORO
I see that.

MILAGROS
You can tell the man I got sick.

TEODORO
He will wait.

MILAGROS
He can wait till Christmas.

TEODORO
(almost smiling)
You are your grandmother.

MILAGROS
Good.

Below them the bus depot's air brakes hiss, and every pigeon in the loft turns its head toward the sound at once. Teodoro unrolls the feed sack, scoops a handful, and holds it out flat. Milagros opens her hand with the bird in it and Pavarotti, left alone in her palm, pecks at the grain on her grandfather's hand.

TEODORO (CONT'D)
Twelve.

MILAGROS
Twelve.

TEODORO
At five o'clock we will tell him eleven.

MILAGROS
We'll tell him ten and a half.

TEODORO
There is no half.

MILAGROS
There is if he doesn't know which one.

Teodoro looks at her a long moment, then at the clock on the wall that stopped years ago, then at the stairwell door still standing open.

TEODORO
Close the door. You will let them out.

She closes it with her heel.
